WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.090 --> 00:00:01.470
<v Instructor>Okay, so welcome to a long</v>

2
00:00:01.470 --> 00:00:04.230
and detailed lecture on VPC Endpoints.

3
00:00:04.230 --> 00:00:07.230
So VPC Endpoints, as you know, allow you to connect

4
00:00:07.230 --> 00:00:09.690
to AWS services using the private network

5
00:00:09.690 --> 00:00:12.390
instead of going through the public internet.

6
00:00:12.390 --> 00:00:14.940
This scale horizontally and are redundant.

7
00:00:14.940 --> 00:00:17.550
So that means that as soon as you're using VPC Endpoints,

8
00:00:17.550 --> 00:00:21.180
you don't need to have an internet gateway or a NAT instance

9
00:00:21.180 --> 00:00:24.330
or NAT Gateway to access AWS services.

10
00:00:24.330 --> 00:00:27.630
Everything goes through the private, internal network.

11
00:00:27.630 --> 00:00:28.740
So how does that work?

12
00:00:28.740 --> 00:00:30.900
We have our VPC and a private subnet.

13
00:00:30.900 --> 00:00:33.390
In our private subnet, we have an EC2 instance.

14
00:00:33.390 --> 00:00:37.680
And say, we're trying to access Amazon S3 or DynamoDB.

15
00:00:37.680 --> 00:00:41.760
In which case, we can set up a VPC endpoint gateway.

16
00:00:41.760 --> 00:00:45.597
So Gateway is only for S3 and DynamoDB.

17
00:00:45.597 --> 00:00:48.030
And if we do so, then our EC2 instance

18
00:00:48.030 --> 00:00:49.950
will be able to talk to S3

19
00:00:49.950 --> 00:00:52.740
and it will be able to talk to DynamoDB.

20
00:00:52.740 --> 00:00:56.100
And for all services, including S3 and DynamoDB,

21
00:00:56.100 --> 00:00:58.713
you can create a VPC endpoint interface.

22
00:00:59.640 --> 00:01:02.280
So for example, say we're trying to access CloudWatch,

23
00:01:02.280 --> 00:01:04.530
then to access CloudWatch privately,

24
00:01:04.530 --> 00:01:07.020
then you need to create a VPC endpoint interface,

25
00:01:07.020 --> 00:01:07.920
which is an ENI.

26
00:01:07.920 --> 00:01:10.620
So it has to live within one of your subnets.

27
00:01:10.620 --> 00:01:13.140
And then your EC2 instance would connect

28
00:01:13.140 --> 00:01:17.280
through that ENI to the CloudWatch service.

29
00:01:17.280 --> 00:01:19.380
In case of issues with VPC endpoints,

30
00:01:19.380 --> 00:01:22.260
you need to check the DNS setting for the resolution

31
00:01:22.260 --> 00:01:24.900
in your VPC, or you check your route tables to make sure

32
00:01:24.900 --> 00:01:27.630
that there is indeed a route from your EC2 instance

33
00:01:27.630 --> 00:01:29.610
to the endpoints.

34
00:01:29.610 --> 00:01:30.810
And it is now possible

35
00:01:30.810 --> 00:01:34.710
to access your VPC endpoints across different regions.

36
00:01:34.710 --> 00:01:36.210
Okay, so now, let's do a deep dive

37
00:01:36.210 --> 00:01:38.160
onto the VPC endpoint gateway.

38
00:01:38.160 --> 00:01:41.220
This only works for Amazon S3 and DynamoDB,

39
00:01:41.220 --> 00:01:44.820
and you must create one gateway per VPC.

40
00:01:44.820 --> 00:01:48.000
You must update route table entries to make it work.

41
00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:49.980
And so that means that it will be applied

42
00:01:49.980 --> 00:01:54.120
to all the instances that are inheriting that route table.

43
00:01:54.120 --> 00:01:56.130
The gateway is defined at the VPC level.

44
00:01:56.130 --> 00:01:59.250
And so for example, if we're getting a VPC endpoint gateway

45
00:01:59.250 --> 00:02:02.100
into Amazon S3 and we have an EC2 instance

46
00:02:02.100 --> 00:02:04.410
that want to access Amazon S3,

47
00:02:04.410 --> 00:02:06.960
then we have to edit the route table

48
00:02:06.960 --> 00:02:08.790
to be looking something like this.

49
00:02:08.790 --> 00:02:11.550
And so as we can see, there's one rule in the middle,

50
00:02:11.550 --> 00:02:14.760
this one that looks very, very important.

51
00:02:14.760 --> 00:02:17.790
So the destination is, okay if you're trying to get

52
00:02:17.790 --> 00:02:22.380
to the Amazon S3 service or these sets of public IP,

53
00:02:22.380 --> 00:02:26.610
which represents, by the way, the public IP of Amazon S3,

54
00:02:26.610 --> 00:02:29.850
then the target should be this VPCE,

55
00:02:29.850 --> 00:02:31.440
which is a VPC endpoint,

56
00:02:31.440 --> 00:02:33.570
and then is going to redirect things

57
00:02:33.570 --> 00:02:35.250
privately through the network.

58
00:02:35.250 --> 00:02:38.220
So your EC2 instance, thanks to this route table,

59
00:02:38.220 --> 00:02:39.240
we'll be able to reach

60
00:02:39.240 --> 00:02:41.790
whenever we are requesting the S3 service,

61
00:02:41.790 --> 00:02:44.160
we'll be able to reach the VPC endpoint gateway

62
00:02:44.160 --> 00:02:46.410
and talk to S3 privately.

63
00:02:46.410 --> 00:02:49.170
For this to work, we need to make sure DNS resolution

64
00:02:49.170 --> 00:02:52.410
is enabled in the VPC, and bonus,

65
00:02:52.410 --> 00:02:55.170
we can use the same public host name for S3.

66
00:02:55.170 --> 00:02:56.340
So we don't need to use IP,

67
00:02:56.340 --> 00:02:58.860
we can use the public host name for Amazon S3.

68
00:02:58.860 --> 00:03:00.600
It'll resolve it to an IP,

69
00:03:00.600 --> 00:03:02.790
and that IP will be in the route table.

70
00:03:02.790 --> 00:03:03.623
And the route table is saying,

71
00:03:03.623 --> 00:03:05.790
"No, don't go to the public internet,

72
00:03:05.790 --> 00:03:09.150
go through the VPC endpoint and things will work."

73
00:03:09.150 --> 00:03:12.180
So this is really cool because now we have full,

74
00:03:12.180 --> 00:03:15.210
private access from EC2 to Amazon S3.

75
00:03:15.210 --> 00:03:18.030
Now, as I said, the VPC endpoint gateway

76
00:03:18.030 --> 00:03:20.550
cannot be extended out of a VPC.

77
00:03:20.550 --> 00:03:23.310
So that means that if you're using a VPN connection,

78
00:03:23.310 --> 00:03:26.640
direct connect, a transit gateway or VPC peering,

79
00:03:26.640 --> 00:03:30.000
this cannot go through your VPC endpoint gateway.

80
00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:32.940
Your VPC endpoint gateway is really only here

81
00:03:32.940 --> 00:03:35.520
for your own VPC, and that's it.

82
00:03:35.520 --> 00:03:36.750
And it's something you have to remember.

83
00:03:36.750 --> 00:03:38.760
You cannot share VPC endpoint gateways

84
00:03:38.760 --> 00:03:41.760
besides what you have within your VPC.

85
00:03:41.760 --> 00:03:44.280
And then we have VPC Endpoint Interface,

86
00:03:44.280 --> 00:03:46.470
and they're a little bit different from Endpoint Gateway

87
00:03:46.470 --> 00:03:48.270
because here we provision an ENI,

88
00:03:48.270 --> 00:03:49.950
so an elastic network interface,

89
00:03:49.950 --> 00:03:53.820
and this ENI will have some private host name.

90
00:03:53.820 --> 00:03:56.730
And the idea is that to do security,

91
00:03:56.730 --> 00:04:00.090
because we have an ENI, we can leverage security groups.

92
00:04:00.090 --> 00:04:02.850
So how do we get access to this ENI

93
00:04:02.850 --> 00:04:04.290
with these private host name.

94
00:04:04.290 --> 00:04:06.390
For this, we need to use a private DNS.

95
00:04:06.390 --> 00:04:07.890
And there is a setting that you have

96
00:04:07.890 --> 00:04:09.870
to enable when you create the endpoint.

97
00:04:09.870 --> 00:04:11.850
And then what they will do is that once you enable

98
00:04:11.850 --> 00:04:15.000
that setting, the public host name of the service

99
00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:18.060
will resolve not to the public IP of that service,

100
00:04:18.060 --> 00:04:19.230
but will resolve instead

101
00:04:19.230 --> 00:04:21.480
to the private endpoint interface host name

102
00:04:21.480 --> 00:04:22.800
that we have created.

103
00:04:22.800 --> 00:04:24.330
And for this to work, we need

104
00:04:24.330 --> 00:04:27.330
to make sure two settings are enabled in our VPC,

105
00:04:27.330 --> 00:04:29.250
which is called enable DNS host name

106
00:04:29.250 --> 00:04:31.920
and enable DNS support. That must be true.

107
00:04:31.920 --> 00:04:34.470
So here's a concrete example for Athena.

108
00:04:34.470 --> 00:04:37.110
If you create a VPC endpoint for Athena,

109
00:04:37.110 --> 00:04:40.380
you will have one, two, three,

110
00:04:40.380 --> 00:04:45.380
and four kind of VPC host names that you can go through.

111
00:04:45.570 --> 00:04:48.030
And each VPC host name is going to redirect

112
00:04:48.030 --> 00:04:51.330
to that ENI, okay, but the last one is the important one.

113
00:04:51.330 --> 00:04:54.270
As you can see, this is the public host name

114
00:04:54.270 --> 00:04:56.220
for Amazon Athena.

115
00:04:56.220 --> 00:04:57.930
But what's going to happen is that

116
00:04:57.930 --> 00:05:01.020
because we have enabled private DNS support,

117
00:05:01.020 --> 00:05:04.230
this private DNS name is going to be a C name

118
00:05:04.230 --> 00:05:06.180
for one of the three things above

119
00:05:06.180 --> 00:05:10.590
which are going to be a A record for your ENI.

120
00:05:10.590 --> 00:05:12.300
And so that means that you don't need

121
00:05:12.300 --> 00:05:13.560
to change your application

122
00:05:13.560 --> 00:05:17.310
to use a private URL if you have enabled private DNS.

123
00:05:17.310 --> 00:05:19.680
But if you haven't enabled private DNS,

124
00:05:19.680 --> 00:05:23.220
then you will have to use one of the three VPC endpoint

125
00:05:23.220 --> 00:05:27.420
full domain name to access Athena privately.

126
00:05:27.420 --> 00:05:30.480
Finally, your interfaces are shareable,

127
00:05:30.480 --> 00:05:33.900
and so they can be accessed from a direct connect connection

128
00:05:33.900 --> 00:05:36.060
or a site-to-site VPN.

129
00:05:36.060 --> 00:05:38.340
So that's it for this lecture on VPC endpoints.

130
00:05:38.340 --> 00:05:39.510
And in the next lecture, we're going

131
00:05:39.510 --> 00:05:41.550
to see VPC endpoint policies.

132
00:05:41.550 --> 00:05:43.173
So see you in the next lecture.

